FFmpeg appears in four places and each of them is a separate build, but all
four are LGPL-only. None of them installs Alpine's `ffmpeg` apk package: that
package is built with `--enable-gpl --enable-version3 --enable-libx264
--enable-libx265` and links libx264 and libx265, i.e. it is GPL-encumbered, not
LGPL-clean. Each image instead builds FFmpeg 9.0.2 from the unmodified upstream
source, pinned by checksum, with `--disable-gpl --disable-nonfree`.

The Video Converter node's image (`CORE/DockerContainers/video-converter/
Dockerfile`) builds FFmpeg 9.0.2 from the unmodified upstream source with
`--disable-gpl --disable-nonfree` and `--enable-libvpx`, and its runtime layer
installs `lame-libs libvorbis opus libvpx openssl python3`: no H.264 encoder
(libx264, libopenh264 or a hardware `h264_*`) is present in the default image,
so CE ships no H.264 anywhere. convert.py probes `ffmpeg -encoders` at runtime:
when no H.264 encoder is present, mp4/mov targets are written with `libvpx-vp9`
video plus FFmpeg's native `aac` encoder, and mov targets are muxed into an
mp4 container (same extension, playable).

The Audio Converter node's image configures:

  ./configure --prefix=/usr/local --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree --disable-doc \
      --enable-libmp3lame --enable-libvorbis --enable-libopus --enable-openssl \
      --enable-shared

That build reports `License: LGPL version 2.1 or later` and enables only
libmp3lame (LGPL), libvorbis (BSD) and libopus (BSD) plus openssl. Verified
against the built image, not against Alpine's packaging policy: `ffmpeg -buildconf`
shows no `--enable-gpl`, no `--enable-nonfree` and no libx264/libx265, and `ldd
$(which ffmpeg)` resolves no libx264/libx265/libfdk_aac.

The FFmpeg_CPU video node's image configures:

  ./configure --prefix=/usr/local --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree --disable-doc \
      --enable-libmp3lame --enable-libvorbis --enable-libopus --enable-libvpx \
      --enable-openssl --enable-static

That build enables libmp3lame (LGPL), libvorbis (BSD), libopus (BSD), libvpx
(BSD — VP8/VP9 encoder) and openssl. `ffmpeg -buildconf` shows no `--enable-gpl`,
no `--enable-nonfree`, and no libx264/libx265.

The Video Converter node's image configures:

  ./configure --prefix=/usr/local --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree --disable-doc \
      --enable-libmp3lame --enable-libvorbis --enable-libopus --enable-libvpx \
      --enable-openssl --enable-shared

That build reports `License: LGPL version 2.1 or later` and enables libmp3lame
(LGPL), libvorbis (BSD), libopus (BSD), libvpx (BSD — VP8/VP9 encoder) and
openssl. `ffmpeg -buildconf` shows no `--enable-gpl`, no `--enable-nonfree` and
no libx264/libx265, and `ldd $(which ffmpeg)` resolves no libx264/libx265/libfdk
_aac; the runtime stage installs only lame-libs/libvorbis/opus/libvpx/openssl/
python3, so no H.264 encoder of any kind is present in the image.

The Image Converter node's image configures:

  ./configure --prefix=/usr/local --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree --disable-doc \
      --enable-libwebp --enable-openssl --enable-shared

That build reports `License: LGPL version 2.1 or later` and enables only
libwebp (BSD) plus openssl; it carries no libmp3lame/libvorbis/libopus/libvpx
since this node only converts still images (png/jpeg/webp/tiff/bmp/gif).
`ffmpeg -buildconf` shows no `--enable-gpl`, no `--enable-nonfree` and no
libx264/libx265, and `ldd $(which ffmpeg)` resolves no libx264/libx265/
libfdk_aac. convert.py falls back to Pillow (HPND / PIL
licence; Alpine py3-pillow recipe license="custom:PIL") for
any input this LGPL build cannot decode, so the node keeps working without
ever reaching for a GPL codec.

Corresponding source for all four builds (LGPL-2.1+ requirement): each image
downloads the unmodified upstream release tarball
<https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz> and verifies it, before
configuring, against sha256
`8c3850283eb25fa026482078a04051e0be17347b09ef81a0849bec15a96e002e`. No MOD-side
patch, `sed` or other source edit is applied to that tarball, so the upstream
tarball plus this checksum pin is the exact source of the binaries shipped in
each image and satisfies the corresponding-source requirement for each build.

Optional H.264 add-on images (opt-in, not installed by default):

Two separate, optional images add H.264 encoding. They are NOT part of the
default install, are not started by the default compose profile set, and do not
replace the default images described above: the default ffmpeg_cpu,
video-converter, audio-converter and image-converter builds stay LGPL-only,
VP9 + AAC, with no H.264 encoder (every statement above about them still holds).
The add-ons are `video-converter-h264` (CORE/DockerContainers/video-converter/
Dockerfile.h264) and `ffmpeg_cpu-h264` (CORE/DockerContainers/FFMpeg_CPU/
Dockerfile.h264), built for linux/amd64 only and pulled only on request of
the user (compose profile `h264`, `docker compose --profile h264 pull`; the user
then turns on the setting `nodes.h264_addon_enabled` and adds the "Video
Converter (H.264)" node; the user can turn the add-on off again in Settings).

  - Runtime: Debian bookworm-slim (glibc), not Alpine, because Cisco's prebuilt
    libopenh264 Linux binary needs glibc and does not load on Alpine/musl.
  - FFmpeg 9.0.2, LGPL-2.1+, built with --disable-gpl --disable-nonfree (no
    libx264, no libx265, no libfdk_aac). Corresponding source: the same
    unmodified upstream tarball <https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-9.0.2.tar.xz>,
    verified against sha256
    `8c3850283eb25fa026482078a04051e0be17347b09ef81a0849bec15a96e002e`; no MOD-side
    patch is applied.
  - H.264 encoding: Cisco OpenH264 2.7.0 (BSD-2-Clause), used as Cisco's
    UNMODIFIED prebuilt binary
    <https://ciscobinary.openh264.org/libopenh264-2.7.0-linux64.8.so.bz2>
    (bz2 sha256 `44ad0dd2c5af2bac84d26f89f2eb4e268708b8739323d15d33837f7221ce4980`,
    soname libopenh264.so.8). It is never rebuilt from source and no distro
    openh264 package is used. The BSD-2 header files come from the v2.7.0 tag of
    <https://github.com/Cisco/openh264>. Both licence texts are installed in the
    image at /usr/share/doc/openh264/ (CISCO_BINARY_LICENSE.txt,
    OPENH264_BSD2_LICENSE.txt). Corresponding source for the binary:
    <http://www.openh264.org> (same BSD terms).
  - Cisco's AVC/H.264 patent coverage (Cisco pays the patent-pool royalties)
    applies ONLY to the binary that Cisco itself builds and distributes. It does
    not extend to a rebuild from source or to a distribution package of OpenH264.
    Cisco's licence also sets conditions on how its binary is delivered and shown
    to end users (reproduced below). H.264 encoding and decoding may require
    patent licences in some jurisdictions.
  - Display text, as Cisco's licence condition 3 states it:
    "OpenH264 Video Codec provided by Cisco Systems, Inc."
    This text is given here and in the node library documentation page
    (CORE/docs-site/docs/node-library.md, Video Converter entry). The setting
    `nodes.h264_addon_enabled` and the "Video Converter (H.264)" node do not
    show it in the product UI itself.
  - Debian base packages in the add-on images (bookworm-slim and the runtime
    libraries the Dockerfiles install: libmp3lame0, libvorbis0a, libvorbisenc2,
    libogg0, libopus0, libvpx7, libssl3; python3 in video-converter-h264; bash is
    part of Debian base) carry their own Debian licences, published on the
    Debian package pages https://packages.debian.org/bookworm/<package> and in
    each package's /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright inside the image. Debian
    base packages include GPL-licensed ones (for example bash and the base
    utilities); they are separate programs in the image, not linked into the
    FFmpeg build or MOD Core code.

